  {% set ubigiPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Both Nomad and Ubigi are built for travelers who need more than a single short trip's worth of data, but they get there in opposite ways. <strong>Nomad</strong> sells a big prepaid bundle once (its Global-EX line, up to a year of validity) and you're done until it runs out. <strong>Ubigi</strong> sells a recurring subscription that renews and charges your card automatically until you cancel it, and its own cancellation terms are stricter than most one-off eSIMs. If you don't want a plan that bills itself again without you doing anything, that difference matters more than either provider's price.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: One Big Bundle vs. an Auto-Renewing Subscription</h2>
  <p>
    Nomad's <strong>Global-EX</strong> bundles run from 10GB to 50GB, stay valid for up to a year, and get cheaper per gigabyte the bigger the bundle: roughly $2.58/GB on the largest ones versus about $5.15/GB on Nomad's standard Global plan. You pay once, use it down over as long as a year, and nothing renews or charges you again unless you buy another bundle yourself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ubigi instead sells recurring plans that renew on their own: a <strong>monthly</strong> plan that auto-charges every month and, per Ubigi's own help center, can't be cancelled until you've held it for at least <strong>three months</strong>, or an <strong>annual</strong> plan that's paid in full upfront and <strong>cannot be cancelled or refunded</strong> during that 12-month term at all. Ubigi does also sell one-off plans with no recurring billing, similar to Nomad's standard plans, but its long-term option specifically is the subscription, not a big prepaid bundle like Global-EX.
  </p>

