  <h2>Is Nomad or Ubigi the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Nomad if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You're traveling or living abroad for months and want to pay once for a bundle that lasts up to a year</li>
        <li>You don't want anything auto-renewing or charging your card again without you buying it</li>
        <li>You want guaranteed hotspot/tethering and 24/7 live chat support</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Ubigi if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You return to the same country regularly enough that a recurring monthly plan genuinely beats buying fresh eSIMs each time</li>
        <li>You'd rather buy from an established MVNO (Ubigi is run by Transatel, part of NTT Group) than a smaller reseller</li>
        <li>You're fine committing to at least 3 months on a monthly plan, or a full non-cancellable year on the annual plan, in exchange for that recurring setup</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ubigi's recurring plans lock you in; Nomad's Global-EX doesn't.</strong> Ubigi's monthly plan can't be cancelled for the first 3 months, and its annual plan can't be cancelled or refunded at all during its 12-month term. Nomad's Global-EX is a straight prepaid purchase: once you've paid for the bundle, there's nothing left to cancel and nothing that bills you again unless you choose to buy more.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Neither provider rolls over unused data, on any plan.</strong> Whatever's left on a Nomad bundle when it expires, or left in a Ubigi billing period when it renews, is gone either way. The difference is how long you have to use it: up to a year on a large Nomad Global-EX bundle, versus one billing cycle at a time on a Ubigi recurring plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Nomad vs Ubigi FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Nomad or Ubigi</h3>
      <p>Nomad, on most destinations we've checked. Ubigi's per-GB price runs above Nomad's standard plans, and Nomad's Global-EX bundles push the per-GB cost down further on long stays in a way Ubigi's recurring plans don't match.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one can I cancel more easily</h3>
      <p>Nomad, because there's nothing to cancel: Global-EX is a one-time prepaid purchase. Ubigi's monthly plan requires a 3-month minimum commitment before you can cancel, and its annual plan can't be cancelled or refunded during its 12-month term at all.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is better for someone who returns to the same country often</h3>
      <p>Ubigi's recurring monthly plan is built for exactly that, refreshing automatically instead of making you buy a fresh eSIM on every trip. Nomad doesn't offer a recurring option; its long-term answer is a large prepaid Global-EX bundle instead.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either company have a notable pedigree</h3>
      <p>Ubigi is a brand of Transatel, a French MVNO enabler operating since the early 2000s and now part of NTT Group, which also supplies the connectivity behind connected-car systems for automakers including BMW, Toyota, and Jeep. Nomad doesn't advertise a similar telecom parent company.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

